# Platform AI (Think)

Before changing anything AI-related, **read `docs/architecture/ai-think.md`.**
It records decisions made on 2026-09-28 that the schema alone does not show.

## Non-negotiables

1. The OpenAI key lives only in the Edge Function secret `OPENAI_API_KEY`.
   Never read it from `platform_config`, never ship it in a Flutter app, never commit it.
2. Apps never call OpenAI directly. Go through `ai_think`, `ai_memo_assist`, or another
   Edge Function that uses `_shared/ai/provider.ts`.
3. AI tools are read tools or propose tools, and go through RLS/RPC checks with the caller's JWT.
   A propose tool only inserts one `ai_actions` row via `ai_action_propose` (conversation splits via
   `ai_conversation_split_propose`); execution happens in
   `ai_action_apply` when the operator approves the card. Do not add a tool that executes a change,
   runs SQL, or touches the schema. New kinds of work = new `ai_actions.kind` + preview + apply branch.
   See `docs/architecture/ai-think-actions.md`.
4. Only a human action writes `ai_memories`. AI output is a draft until the operator
   presses approve. Never auto-promote a draft to `active`. Think's memory cards (`save_memory`,
   `memory_card_change`) are proposals too; the memory is written only in `ai_memory_action_apply`.
5. Model names, reasoning effort, token limits, and prices come from `_shared/ai/config.ts`
   (env overrides). Do not hardcode a model name at a call site.
6. Every AI call records one `ai_runs` row via `recordAiRun` (it never throws).
   Keep `feature` names stable; the usage screen groups by them.
7. Keep the context order in `ai_think/context.ts` stable (instructions, split summary for split
   conversations, history, work-state note, feature-status note, relevant-memory note, current message).
   Reordering breaks prompt caching.
8. Update memories with the optimistic `version` check. An empty update result means
   someone else saved first; reload instead of overwriting.
9. Tests use `FakeAiProvider` (`AI_PROVIDER=fake`) and never assert exact AI wording.
10. Bump the `*_PROMPT_VERSION` constant in `ai_think/prompts.ts` or
    `ai_memo_assist/tasks.ts` when a prompt changes.
11. The conversation tree (`ai_tree_nodes`) is not answer context. The only AI read is the
    `list_tree_folders` tool: folder ids/titles/parents and conversation placement, plus conversation
    titles via `list_conversations`. Never expose excerpt titles, summaries, or message text.
    Messages with `context_excluded = true` must stay out of every AI read path
    (`store.listMessages` is the single place that reads messages for AI).
12. Keep code diffs out of AI context. Store queries select individual `result` fields
    (`ai_think/store.ts` `ROUND_COLUMNS`); only the code review sees a clipped diff head.
13. Conversation split (`ai-think-actions.md` §7): the server picks the range (the proposing turn's user
    message and its completed answer); never take message ids from the model. Approval runs in
    `ai_conversation_split_apply`, which locks the conversation row before the action row, like the
    message insert trigger. The split summary is reference context, never a memory.
14. Feature status (`ai_think/features.ts`) is computed per request from the Edge, DB
    (`ai_think_db_features`, read-only) and app (`client.features`) versions. Expose a feature's tool
    only when it is `ready`; a failed DB check is `unknown`, never ready. Never probe a missing RPC with
    a write call.
15. Roles and material scope (`ai-think-actions.md` §8, feature `think_roles`): every AI read of memories and
    documents goes through `AccessContext` (`ai_think/access_context.ts`) with the role stored on the
    conversation; only active memories reach the AI. Never take a role or permission from the app for an
    existing conversation (`body.role` only seeds a new one). Document bodies go to the AI only when the
    document is explicitly linked, within the budget. Never auto-reclassify legacy memories.
16. Memory proposals (`ai-think-actions.md` §9, feature `memory_proposals`): Think may propose only decisions
    and principles the operator explicitly confirmed, at most one memory card (save or change) per answer
    (enforced on the Edge). Duplicate checks run in the DB at propose and again at approve. Memory cards do
    not supersede each other and pending cards stay until the operator acts; never auto-withdraw or
    auto-replace one. Not offered in relay mode.

## Code investigation (Think ↔ Cursor)

Design and current phase: `docs/architecture/ai-code-bridge.md`.

- Investigate and plan runs (`mode = 'investigate' | 'plan'`) get read tools only (`read`, `grep`, `glob`, `ls`, SDK mode `plan`).
  Change runs (`mode = 'change'`) add `edit` and `delete` only, run in an isolated git worktree of a
  working-tree snapshot, and set `CODE_BRIDGE_WRITE_ROOT` so the hook blocks every path outside it.
  Never add shell, MCP, PR, commit, or push tools. The one exception is the worker's own `ask_think`
  customTool in investigate/plan runs (tools `READ_ONLY_TOOLS + 'mcp'`, see "Cursor asks Think" below).
  The real working folder changes only through an
  approved apply job (`git apply --check` first, backup ref before, revert with `-R`).
- `CURSOR_API_KEY` and `CODE_BRIDGE_WORKER_TOKEN` live only in the operator PC's environment.
  The server keeps only `CODE_BRIDGE_WORKER_TOKEN_SHA256`. Never put either in an app, the DB, or git.
- Request state changes go through the `ai_code_request_*` (app) and `ai_code_bridge_*` (worker,
  service role) RPCs. The app writes only draft `title` and `request`.
- `.cursor/hooks/deny-secrets.mjs` must stay fail-closed. Korean Windows can hand it input with
  quotes eaten after Korean text; it then checks path fields only. Keep its tests in
  `tools/code_bridge/test/deny_secrets.test.mjs` passing. The `preToolUse` matcher must include
  `Write|Delete` (that is how the SDK reports edits and deletes).
- Code review (`ai_code_bridge/code_review.ts`) posts at most one assistant message per request and
  asks follow-up rounds only while round < max_rounds (investigate: 2, plan: 3). It never applies anything.
- Code changes go through coordination: Think calls `start_code_plan`, which starts a read-only plan
  request without approval (the one exception to propose-then-approve; it still counts against the daily
  limit and is recorded as an `applied` `code_plan` action). After the last round, a 조율본 with instructions
  and no `decisions` starts the change run itself (`ai_code_bridge_start_change`, `preview.auto`); one with
  `decisions` becomes a `code_change` card the operator answers first. If the daily limit blocks the auto
  start, fall back to the card. Do not let plan mode edit files.
- Roles (2026-10-07): the operator owns everything, Think acts as CEO, Cursor as staff. The operator
  approves only the working-folder apply and production DB/deploy. Never auto-apply, never push
  migrations, never deploy from the code bridge.
- After a change run the worker runs fixed checks in the copy (`tools/code_bridge/src/checks.mjs`):
  flutter analyze/test, deno check/test, worker tests, migration numbering, and `tools/db_test` (PGlite,
  in-memory; never the production DB). Statuses are passed / failed / skipped (미실행) / preexisting;
  never report skipped as passed. Checks get an env with keys and tokens removed. On a failure the
  review may start at most 2 re-fix requests (`ai_code_bridge_fix`, `request.fix_of`), each from the
  previous diff, each counted against the daily limit; then it reports to the operator in plain words.
- To build on a change that is not applied to the working folder yet, Think passes
  `continue_from_request_id` to `start_code_plan` (`spec.continue_from`). An id in the request text alone
  gives Cursor nothing. At claim the server sends that request's final patch as `base_patch`
  (`ai_code_bridge_patch_source`: final status, last round only, complete, not superseded by a re-fix);
  the worker applies it on a fresh snapshot copy and fails with the reason or conflict files instead of
  guessing. Never log patch bodies, and never ask the operator to copy a diff by hand.
- Timed-out change patches (2026-10-10, `ai-think-actions.md` §4.7.1): a `needs_review` last round with
  `result.partial = 'timeout'` (or, for old rows, a summary starting "30분 안에 끝나지 않아 멈췄습니다") can
  be continued, never applied. The worker first checks the patch applies to its own `base_commit`; the
  continued request is labeled "미완성 패치에서 시작" and its final diff is checked as a whole. Other
  `needs_review` rows stay rejected.
- New migrations that change AI RPCs need a `tools/db_test/test` case. Keep migration versions above the
  current maximum and unique.
- Unresolved points in a 조율본 are `spec.decisions` (multiple choice, recommended option first). The operator
  answers them in the card; `ai_code_plan_revise` records the answers as `owner_answers` plus
  "운영자 결정 — " constraints and re-runs one plan round. Keep those constraints when writing a new 조율본,
  and never treat a recommended option as the operator's answer.
- Work effort (2026-10-10, `docs/architecture/ai-code-bridge.md` §7.1): the default worker model is
  `grok-4.7`; each request carries `request.effort` (medium/high/xhigh) and `effort_reason`, picked by Think
  and changeable on the card before approval. Empty or unknown means `high`; never treat Think's pick as
  certainly right. The worker resolves it against `Cursor.models.list()` (closest supported value) and
  records the actual model and effort on the round. Effort never changes mode meanings or permissions.

## Cursor asks Think (relay, 2026-10-10)

Design: `docs/architecture/ai-think.md` §12.

- Cursor reads Think only through `ai_think_relay` (IDE, MCP server `tools/think_relay`) and the worker's
  `ask_think` (via `ai_code_bridge`). Reads are active memories, conversation lists and bodies (never
  `context_excluded` messages; reuse `store.listMessages`), and code request summaries without diffs.
  Never add a relay action that writes memories, tree nodes, actions, or code requests.
- In relay mode Think gets read tools only (`thinkToolsFor({readOnly: true})`), and an acting tool call is
  refused without running. IDE asks continue only `origin = 'cursor'` conversations.
- The verdict is decided by the server (`relay_verdict.ts`), not by the model: `answered` needs at least one
  currently active basis memory and no owner questions; everything else is `needs_owner`. Keep it fail-closed.
  Cursor must not proceed on a `needs_owner` answer.
- Limits live in `ai_relay_reserve` (KST daily limit, worker: running request it owns, investigate/plan only,
  3 per round). Do not count limits in the client only.
- The relay token is compared to `THINK_RELAY_TOKEN_SHA256`; the token itself stays on the operator PC.
  Register the MCP server in the user-level `~/.cursor/mcp.json`, never in the repo `.cursor/mcp.json`
  (the worker loads project settings, and it turns `ask_think` off if that file exists).

## Out of scope unless the user asks

Autonomous DB writes, auto-running migrations, arbitrary SQL, Codex integration,
vector databases, complex model routers, unapproved applies to the working folder, commits or PRs by the Cursor agent.
Multi-agent work is limited to the code bridge above.
